#=GF RN [1]
#=GF RM 11839493
#=GF RT Towards the structure of the mammalian signal recognition particle.
#=GF RA Wild K, Weichenrieder O, Strub K, Sinning I, Cusack S
#=GF RL Curr Opin Struct Biol 2002;12:72-81.
#=GF RN [2]
#=GF RM 10678824
#=GF RT Crystal structure of the ribonucleoprotein core of the signal recognition
#=GF RT particle.
#=GF RA Batey RT, Rambo RP, Lucast L, Rha B, Doudna JA
#=GF RL Science 2000;287:1232-1239.
#=GF RN [3]
#=GF RM 11243816
#=GF RT Structural and energetic analysis of RNA recognition by a universally
#=GF RT conserved protein from the signal recognition particle.
#=GF RA Batey RT, Sagar MB, Doudna JA
#=GF RL J Mol Biol 2001;307:229-246.
#=GF RN [4]
#=GF RM 12520023
#=GF RT SRPDB: Signal Recognition Particle Database.
#=GF RA Rosenblad MA, Gorodkin J, Knudsen B, Zwieb C, Samuelsson T
#=GF RL Nucleic Acids Res 2003;31:363-364.
#=GF RN [5]
#=GF RM 12140321
#=GF RT Prediction of signal recognition particle RNA genes.
#=GF RA Regalia M, Rosenblad MA, Samuelsson T
#=GF RL Nucleic Acids Res 2002;30:3368-3377.
#=GF RN [6]
#=GF RM 19838050
#=GF RT Kinship in the SRP RNA family.
#=GF RA Rosenblad MA, Larsen N, Samuelsson T, Zwieb C
#=GF RL RNA Biol. 2009;6:508-516.
#=GF CC The signal recognition particle (SRP) is a universally conserved
#=GF CC ribonucleoprotein. It is involved in the co-translational targeting of
#=GF CC proteins to membranes. The eukaryotic SRP consists of a 300-nucleotide 7S
#=GF CC RNA and six proteins: SRPs 72, 68, 54, 19, 14, and 9. Archaeal SRP
#=GF CC consists of a 7S RNA and homologues of the eukaryotic SRP19 and SRP54
#=GF CC proteins. Eukaryotic and archaeal 7S RNAs have very similar secondary
#=GF CC structures, as represented by this family. Eight helical elements fold
#=GF CC into the Alu and S domains, separated by a long linker region [1,2]. The
#=GF CC Alu domain is thought to mediate the peptide chain elongation retardation
#=GF CC function of the SRP [1]. The universally conserved helix which interacts
#=GF CC with the SRP54 M domain mediates signal sequence recognition [2,3]. In
#=GF CC eukaryotes and archaea, the SRP19-helix 6 complex is thought to be
#=GF CC involved in SRP assembly and stabilises helix 8 for SRP54 binding [1]. The
#=GF CC Signal Recognition Particle Database (SRPDB) [4] provides compilations of
#=GF CC SRP components, with phylogenetic data and structural illustrations. Many
#=GF CC sequences below the threshold for this family are clearly closely related,
#=GF CC but are most likely to be SRP RNA derived pseudogenes. The human genome in
#=GF CC particular is known to contain a large amount of SRP RNA related sequence,
#=GF CC including Alu repeats. While the threshold of this family is set high so
#=GF CC as to minimise the number of hits to such regions, it is likely that some
#=GF CC remain.
#=GF WK Signal_recognition_particle_RNA
#=GF SQ 91

